{:edb/db-before-t 843 :edb/db-after-t 844 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534156 50 #inst "2026-10-06T05:40:32.228Z" 13194139534156 true] [17592186100566 1100 "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0$, where $x$ is measured in degrees." 13194139534156 false] [17592186100566 1100 "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees." 13194139534156 true] [17592186100566 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/5f32529ce4132c594d8a886222ff6f1a356eefa95fa4c7e1fd38b9bd4e3932a9.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,360)$ are $x=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186100566 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/8584042d6ef9bb2998a7c62ae2b7e609.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 180^{∘} + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,360)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$" 13194139534156 false] [17592186100567 1100 "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1$?" 13194139534156 false] [17592186100567 1100 "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1?$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186100567 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan x &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left(1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi$. Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is:\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 false] [17592186100567 1145 "Recall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left( 1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi.$  Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is: \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186100568 1100 "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0$?" 13194139534156 false] [17592186100568 1100 "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0?$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186100568 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\sin x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,2\\pi)$ are $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186100568 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\csc x + 2\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\csc x &= -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\csc x &= -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_{1} = \\arcsin (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = \\frac{5π}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin x< 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/55452604d3a171e9228cc769aae317ea.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in[0,2\\pi)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 false] [17592186100569 1100 "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}$. What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/42b069cebd215708e6a018e0e3ecc90d.png)" 13194139534156 false] [17592186100569 1100 "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/cae8d73bdf9a21e956c444db4b22bfc26e20b7a9d1593d7759fc119aa4c6a623.png)" 13194139534156 true] [17592186100569 1145 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos x} &= \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos x > 0$, the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1258/Source/Images/a8b3e42e6690c60434773d04d3b5d73d.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $[MATH: x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)]$ are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 false] [17592186100569 1145 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nRecall that $\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x >   0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)$ are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ , \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186100732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279179 13194139534156 true] [17592186100732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279186 13194139534156 true] [17592186100732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279221 13194139534156 true] [17592186100732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279228 13194139534156 true] [17592186100732 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279256 13194139534156 true] [17592186100733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279271 13194139534156 true] [17592186100733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279276 13194139534156 true] [17592186100733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279282 13194139534156 true] [17592186100733 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279302 13194139534156 true] [17592186100734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279193 13194139534156 true] [17592186100734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279200 13194139534156 true] [17592186100734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279207 13194139534156 true] [17592186100734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279263 13194139534156 true] [17592186100734 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279295 13194139534156 true] [17592186100735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279214 13194139534156 true] [17592186100735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279235 13194139534156 true] [17592186100735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279242 13194139534156 true] [17592186100735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279249 13194139534156 true] [17592186100735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279287 13194139534156 true] [17592186101147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534156 true] [17592186101147 1100 "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186101147 1100 "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$. Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534156 false] [17592186101147 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-{60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279180 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1089 "q-120762" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1100 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1113 #uuid "db88ade5-9f00-5732-8750-043df145e23b" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279179 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279180 1000 #edb/ref 175