6 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534156 true] [17592186279186 1100 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186279186 1113 #uuid "47d5d3d5-2893-5d8a-9d82-fbb4c7b2b493" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279186 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x={\\theta }^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},$ where $\\theta \\in [0,360),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279188 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279189 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279190 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279191 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279192 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1041 "selection" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279189 13194139534156 true] [17592186279187 1110 #uuid "56b66743-d5b2-5446-853e-ba7c11915bf0" 13194139