 true] [17592186279282 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279285 13194139534156 true] [17592186279282 1089 "q-323846" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279282 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534156 true] [17592186279282 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279282 1113 #uuid "ef4ae1e3-38bc-5f9b-bb58-70f2ac357b19" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279282 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing this as $x=\\theta +{180}^{∘}\\,n,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279284 13194139534156 true] [17592186279283 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534156 true] [17592186279283 1041 "field-1" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279283 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279284 13194139534156 true] [17592186279283 1110 #uuid "be0dd745-a6b1-5891-b1f7-6f119c4a0f68" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279284 1034 "135" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279284 1111 #uuid "f9ee1abc-8012-5961-ab5a-30f63b4709a9" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279286 13194139534156 true] [17592186279285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534156 true] [17592186279285 1041 "field-2" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279286 13194139534156 true] [17592186279285 1110 #uuid "7024a22e-f0cc-5ff4-8b96-f913cb795b5e" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279286 1034 "180" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279286 1111 #uuid "9cc414bc-0d9f-565e-af0a-ac05455a3868" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279288 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279291 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279293 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1089 "q-324056" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1113 #uuid "f54ae505-1f6b-5a00-9a72-c2517a389f06" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279287 1145 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279289 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279290 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1041 "field-1" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279290 13194139534156 true] [17592186279288 1110 #uuid "b93d35b8-5820-545e-9fb6-8de12373185d" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279289 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279289 1111 #uuid "57b57e17-a515-5f1c-8c07-30b4688a92fc" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279290 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279290 1111 #uuid "1b05cdd7-c227-5fa2-9192-e4c9321631e0" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279291 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279292 13194139534156 true] [17592186279291 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534156 true] [17592186279291 1041 "field-2" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279291 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279292 13194139534156 true] [17592186279291 1110 #uuid "d37e995b-ec92-5afe-97ca-63af004855f2" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279292 1034 "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279292 1111 #uuid "58bc1b25-f930-520a-afcc-24ac64ddbc54" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279294 13194139534156 true] [17592186279293 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534156 true] [17592186279293 1041 "field-3" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279294 13194139534156 true] [17592186279293 1110 #uuid "5df63614-fd54-5a09-b46b-1b7f2a85f3c8" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279294 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279294 1111 #uuid "68070cdc-8f00-5169-af3b-cba7c8ae8484" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279296 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1089 "q-76736" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1100 "What is the general solution to the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1113 #uuid "c6d74ee2-ab7f-5432-8051-c19d5ab9bb86" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279295 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/87b3a90142f4c810433bd2c219382379fc508fa426567708abddb744f39771c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279297 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279298 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279299 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279300 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279301 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1041 "selection" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279299 13194139534156 true] [17592186279296 1110 #uuid "3423f75c-5bc3-5b0b-8f9d-41bad4fec7b0" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279297 1034 "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " 13194139534156 true] [17592186279297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279297 1111 #uuid "2ea7cf4b-ccc8-543a-a86e-d0cd67f54b79" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279298 1034 "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " 13194139534156 true] [17592186279298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279298 1111 #uuid "2a5cd8e4-3c5f-5dca-8ccb-3e18af1ac9d7" 13194139534156 true] [17592186279299 1034 "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " 13194139534156 true] [17592186279299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534156 true] [17592186279299 1111 #uuid "e62fd867-b2c